# A failed evaluation wedged its root

**DONE.** Found while building `tasks/086`, fixed in the same branch.
It is the fifth of this repo's named failure mode - a wrong answer
that looks exactly like a right one - and the first that was not an
emitter skipping something.

## What it was

libexpr caches the files a FAILED evaluation read, and the cache is
what answers next time. So a file fixed after a bad save keeps raising
the OLD error.

Measured before anything was designed:

    root.nix = "1 + 1"     eval -> 2
    forget_file(root)
    root.nix = "1 + \"     eval -> NixError: syntax error
    cached_files()         still holds root.nix
    root.nix = "2 + 2"     eval -> NixError: syntax error   <- 2:1
    forget_file(root)
    root.nix = "2 + 2"     eval -> 4

The fifth line is the defect. The file on disk reads `2 + 2` and the
evaluator reports a syntax error at 2:1, about text that is not there
any more.

## Why nothing caught it

`Watcher` only forgets through `changed()`, and `changed()` needs a
SNAPSHOT to know which roots a path implicates. A failed evaluation
records no snapshot - `eval_file` raises before the recording line -
so a wedged root is invisible to every later change.

The wedge is therefore PERMANENT, and it is silent in the way this
repo cares about: the caller gets a confident error rather than an
absence, and re-saving the file does not clear it. Only an explicit
`forget_file` does, and nothing was calling one.

`tasks/083`'s seven gates could not see it. Every one of them
evaluates files that parse.

## The fix, and why it costs nothing

`Watcher.eval_file` forgets what a failed evaluation cached.

What to forget is DERIVED rather than measured with an extra call.
Every file a SUCCESSFUL evaluation cached is in that root's snapshot,
so a file that is cached and in no snapshot was read by an evaluation
that failed. That is `_drop_unrecorded`, and it needs only the
`cached_files()` call the success path already makes.

The direction of the error matters. Forgetting a file that was
actually fine costs one re-read; leaving a bad one costs the answer.
So the rule errs towards forgetting.

It is a REPAIR and not a reset, which is the second gate: a root that
evaluated successfully keeps its snapshot across someone else's
failure, because its files are in that snapshot and the rule only
touches what is not.

## Broken on purpose

With the cleanup removed:

    FAILED tests/test_watch.py::test_a_failed_evaluation_does_not_wedge_the_root
    huggorm_bindings.errors.NixError: error: syntax error, unexpected end of file
    FAILED tests/test_warm.py::test_a_root_that_does_not_parse_is_recorded_not_raised
    2 failed, 324 passed, 10 deselected

Two gates, at two layers. The watcher one shows the stale error
directly; the warmer one shows the consequence - an eager pass that
never recovers the root however many times the file is fixed.

## What it says about the others

`tasks/073`, `075`, `078` and `082` were all an EMITTER skipping what
it did not recognise. This one is a CACHE keeping what it should have
dropped, which is the same shape from the other side: state that
outlives the reason it was correct, with nothing in the interface to
say so.

Worth remembering when the next layer caches something.
